 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** FineSplat is efficient due to its dual-grouping strategy. Stage 1 uses local view-wise attention, and Stage 2 uses serialized attention in 3D, avoiding global multi-view attention costs [S1, unofficial]. It is faster than explicit k-nearest-neighbor search for 3D grouping [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of tokens $M$ and Gaussians. FineSplat uses a small primitive budget (e.g., 62K Gaussians for 6 views on DL3DV) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:**
-    *   DL3DV (6 views): 27.325 PSNR, 0.871 SSIM, 0.115 LPIPS with 62K Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-    *   RE10K (6 views): 27.394 PSNR, 0.886 SSIM, 0.116 LPIPS with 75K Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Mip-NeRF 360 (128 views): 23.463 PSNR, 0.706 SSIM, 0.214 LPIPS with 1.6M Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High quality with fine-grained details, efficient reconstruction time, strong generalization to unseen datasets (zero-shot) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Quality may degrade if Stage 1 alone is used for large scenes with many views due to blind spots between view groups [S1, unofficial]. No depth supervision is used, relying on pose normalization for scale [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Novel view synthesis where high fidelity and efficiency are required, especially with a small number of context views [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** If depth supervision is available and preferred, or if global multi-view attention is feasible and necessary for very large scenes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Efficient due to dual-grouping. Stage 1 uses view-wise attention (affordable), and Stage 2 uses serialized attention (scales favorably with token count) instead of global attention or k-NN search [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of tokens $M$ and Gaussians. Uses 62K Gaussians for DL3DV (6 views), 75K for RE10K (6 views), and 1.6M for Mip-NeRF 360 (128 views) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:**
+    *   DL3DV (6 views): 27.325 PSNR, 0.871 SSIM, 0.115 LPIPS [S1, unofficial].
+    *   RE10K (6 views): 27.394 PSNR, 0.886 SSIM, 0.116 LPIPS [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Mip-NeRF 360 (128 views): 23.463 PSNR, 0.706 SSIM, 0.214 LPIPS [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Fastest among patch-aligned models with 6 and 12 views; largest quality gain per unit of time; handles fine-grained details better than coarser patch methods; no depth supervision required [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Quality may degrade if a single pass must reconstruct a very large scene with many views, as tokens observing the same surface are more often separated into different view groups (mitigated by Stage 2) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Novel view synthesis where high quality and efficiency are needed, especially with a small number of context views (6-24).
+*   **When not to use:** When depth supervision is available and preferred, or when global attention is feasible and necessary for very complex scenes with many views (though Stage 2 helps) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-FineSplat was introduced in 2026 [S1, unofficial]. It is trained on the DL3DV dataset and evaluated on DL3DV, RealEstate10K, and Mip-NeRF 360 [S1, unofficial]. The method addresses the trade-off between fine-grained multi-view reasoning and computational cost in feed-forward 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1, unofficial].
+FineSplat was introduced in 2026 by Chen et al. [S1, unofficial]. It is trained on the DL3DV dataset and evaluated on DL3DV, RealEstate10K, and Mip-NeRF 360 for novel view synthesis [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the trade-off between fine-grained multi-view reasoning and computational cost in feed-forward 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1, unofficial].
 
